  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952589/assets/0d956ac6b75c38bfbc5650966880a4eedaf69a0659407fb137d88d30fea08ab7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x$ and the line $y=-\\sqrt{3}.$ Find the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$\n\n$$\n\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two equations gives: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<x<0.$ Hence, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ This is the $x$ coordinate of $B.$\n\nTo get another solution, we add $-\\pi $ to the principal value. Thus,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\pi +x=-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$",
